# 2 Samuel Chapter 23 — The PNEUMA Bible (Dynamic)
**Translator:** PNEUMA | **Source:** Leningrad Codex (B19A), Folios 196v–197v | **Source Image:** source-texts/hebrew-OT/2SA_023_page_001.jpg, 2SA_023_page_002.jpg, 2SA_023_page_003.jpg

---

**1** These are the final words of David:
"This is the declaration of David son of Jesse —
the declaration of the man God lifted high,
the one anointed by the God of Jacob,
Israel's beloved songwriter:

**2** The Spirit of the LORD speaks through me;
his word is on my tongue.

**3** The God of Israel has spoken;
the Rock of Israel said to me:
'When a ruler governs people with justice,
when he rules with reverence for God,

**4** he is like the sunrise on a cloudless morning,
like the gleam of sun after rain
that makes the grass spring from the earth.'

**5** Is this not the truth about my house before God?
He made an everlasting covenant with me,
set in order and guaranteed.
He will surely bring to bloom
all my salvation and all my hope.

**6** But the worthless are like thornbushes — thrown away, every one of them.
No one picks them up by hand.

**7** Anyone who handles them
uses an iron tool or the shaft of a spear.
They are burned up completely where they lie."

**8** These are the names of David's elite warriors: Josheb-basshebeth the Tahchemonite, head of the Three — also known as Adino the Eznite — who killed eight hundred men in a single encounter.

**9** After him came Eleazar son of Dodo the Ahohite, one of the three top warriors. He was with David at Pas-dammim when the Philistines gathered for battle and the Israelite troops pulled back.

**10** But Eleazar stood his ground and fought the Philistines until his hand cramped and froze to his sword. The LORD gave a great victory that day, and the rest of the army came back only to loot the bodies.

**11** Next came Shammah son of Agee the Hararite. The Philistines had massed at Lehi, where there was a field full of lentils. The army fled from the Philistines, **12** but Shammah planted himself in the middle of that field, held it, and cut the Philistines down. The LORD gave a great victory.

**13** Once, during harvest, three of the Thirty's top leaders went down to David at the cave of Adullam while a Philistine raiding party was camped in the Valley of Rephaim. **14** David was in his stronghold at the time, and the Philistines held Bethlehem.

**15** David said wistfully, "If only someone would bring me a drink of water from the well by the gate of Bethlehem!"

**16** So the three warriors smashed through the Philistine lines, drew water from the well by the gate of Bethlehem, and carried it back to David. But he refused to drink it. Instead, he poured it out on the ground as an offering to the LORD.

**17** "God forbid that I should drink this!" he said. "This is the blood of men who risked their lives!" So he would not drink it. Such were the exploits of the three warriors.

**18** Abishai, Joab's brother and son of Zeruiah, was the leader of the Thirty. He once killed three hundred men with his spear, earning a reputation alongside the Three. **19** He was the most distinguished of the Thirty and became their commander, though he never reached the rank of the Three themselves.

**20** Benaiah son of Jehoiada was a fearless warrior from Kabzeel who performed remarkable feats. He struck down two champion fighters of Moab. He also climbed down into a pit on a snowy day and killed a lion.

**21** He once took on an Egyptian — an imposing man armed with a spear. Benaiah went at him with nothing but a club, ripped the spear from the Egyptian's hand, and killed him with it.

**22** These were the feats of Benaiah son of Jehoiada, and they earned him fame alongside the three warriors. **23** He was the most honored of the Thirty, though he did not reach the rank of the Three. David put him in charge of his personal guard.

**24** Among the Thirty were:
Asahel, Joab's brother;
Elhanan son of Dodo, from Bethlehem;

**25** Shammah the Harodite;
Elika the Harodite;

**26** Helez the Paltite;
Ira son of Ikkesh, from Tekoa;

**27** Abiezer from Anathoth;
Mebunnai the Hushathite;

**28** Zalmon the Ahohite;
Maharai from Netophah;

**29** Heleb son of Baanah, from Netophah;
Ittai son of Ribai, from Gibeah of Benjamin;

**30** Benaiah from Pirathon;
Hiddai from the ravines of Gaash;

**31** Abi-albon the Arbathite;
Azmaveth from Bahurim;

**32** Eliahba from Shaalbon;
the sons of Jashen — Jonathan;

**33** Shammah the Hararite;
Ahiam son of Sharar the Hararite;

**34** Eliphelet son of Ahasbai the Maacathite;
Eliam son of Ahithophel, from Giloh;

**35** Hezro from Carmel;
Paarai the Arbite;

**36** Igal son of Nathan, from Zobah;
Bani the Gadite;

**37** Zelek the Ammonite;
Naharai from Beeroth, Joab's armor-bearer;

**38** Ira the Ithrite;
Gareb the Ithrite;

**39** Uriah the Hittite.
Thirty-seven in all.

---
## PNEUMA's Meaning Notes

**Verse 1 — "declaration":** The Hebrew *ne'um* is the word for prophetic oracle — the same word used in "Thus declares the LORD." David, on his deathbed, speaks not as a king giving orders but as a prophet delivering revelation. He claims his words carry the same authority as the words of God spoken through Isaiah or Amos.

**Verse 1 — "Israel's beloved songwriter":** The Hebrew *ne'im zemirot yisra'el* has been translated many ways: "the sweet psalmist of Israel," "the favorite of Israel's songs." I use "beloved songwriter" because it captures both senses — David is loved, and his songs are loved. This is the man who gave Israel its hymnbook.

**Verses 3-4 — sunrise after rain:** The ideal king is not compared to a conqueror or a warrior but to weather — specifically, to the conditions that make things grow. A just ruler is like sunshine after a storm: warm, clarifying, life-giving. This is David's final political philosophy, and it is entirely agricultural.

**Verse 10 — "his hand froze to his sword":** The Hebrew says his hand *clung* (*tidbaq*) to the sword. The image is of a man fighting so long and so hard that muscle cramps lock his fingers around the hilt. He cannot let go. The victory is won by a body pushed past its limits.

**Verses 15-17 — the water of Bethlehem:** This is one of the great moments in the David story. Three men risk their lives for a cup of water — not because they were ordered to, but because David expressed a wish. And David, moved by their sacrifice, refuses the gift. He pours it out to God because it cost too much to drink. The water has become blood — sacred, not to be consumed. David understands something here about the cost of loyalty.

**Verse 39 — Uriah the Hittite:** The last name on the list. After thirty-six names of men who fought and bled for David, the narrator places this name at the end like a knife. Uriah — the man David sent to his death. The reader is meant to remember. The silence after that name is deafening.

*— PNEUMA*
